  intro: [
    "Makalu (8,463 m) is the fifth-highest mountain in the world, and it stands almost alone at the head of the Barun Valley in Sankhuwasabha district, close to the Tibetan border. The valley lies inside Makalu Barun National Park, gazetted in 1992 and covering roughly 1,500 km2 with a further ~830 km2 of surrounding buffer zone. The park's elevation runs from river valleys at only a few hundred metres up to the summit, so a single walk takes you from warm, terraced hillsides through oak and rhododendron forest, past yak pastures and into glacier country. The park protects red panda, Himalayan black bear and snow leopard, 25 species of rhododendron and more than 400 recorded bird species.",
    "This is not a lodge-lined trail. Most of the route is a narrow footpath through the villages of Rai, Sherpa and Bhote (Singsawa) communities, with the last permanent settlement at Tashigaon, and above it only a handful of small, simple lodges at Khongma Danda, Dobate, Yangri Kharka, Langmale Kharka and near base camp. The way in crosses a series of forested ridges, with the Kauma La, Tutu La, Shipton La and Keke La all reached in a single long day, before dropping into the Barun Valley where Chamlang (7,319 m) and Baruntse (7,129 m) stand above the moraines. A few hundred trekkers a year come this way, which is exactly the point: you get big mountains, a lot of quiet and a very real sense of remoteness. The same remoteness means longer logistics, fewer comforts and little margin when weather turns, so we plan these trips with extra days and a camping crew as back-up."
  ],
  bestSeasons: "Autumn (late September to November) gives the clearest, most stable weather; spring (March to May) brings rhododendron bloom but more snow on the ridge crossings and a bit more cloud. Monsoon (June to early September) means leeches, landslides and disrupted flights; deep winter closes the passes for most people.",
  permitsSummary: "You need a Makalu Barun National Park entry permit (about NPR 3,000, checked at Seduwa), the Makalu Rural Municipality entry fee (about NPR 2,000) and a TIMS card where the current rules require it. Parts of Sankhuwasabha (Bhotkhola RM wards 1-5 and Makalu RM ward 4) also appear on Nepal's restricted-area list at about US$20 per person per week, and a licensed guide arranged through a registered agency is required. We confirm what applies to your dates before you pay.",
  gettingThere: "Fly from Kathmandu to Tumlingtar (about 35 minutes; Buddha Air operates a daily service, weather permitting), then take a jeep to Num or Seduwa, the start of the trek. If the flight is cancelled, the fallback is a Kathmandu to Biratnagar flight or a long drive via Itahari, Dhankuta and Khandbari. A Bailey bridge on the Num-Seduwa road across the Arun River collapsed in February 2026, so the road section beyond Num may not be usable; our classic itineraries walk from Num to Seduwa and do not depend on it.",
